[{"record_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000152","candidate_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000152","source_record_id":"rec_000152","source_corpus":"include","title":"H2O: A Foundation Model Bridging Histopathology to Spatial Multi-Omics Profiling","abstract":"Spatial omics technologies have revolutionized the molecular profiling of tissues but remain constrained by high costs and limited scalability. While hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) staining is ubiquitous, it lacks molecular specificity. Here, we present H2O (Histopathology to Omics), a generalist AI framework that bridges the modality gap between histopathology and spatial multi-omics, enabling the direct inference of spatial transcriptomics (ST) and proteomics (SP) landscapes from routine H&amp;E images. H2O integrates Vision Transformers (ViT) with Large Language Models (LLM) via contrastive learning to align histological morphology with semantic molecular knowledge. This cross-modal approach allows the model to incorporate spatial expression profiles into histological pattern recognition, effectively decoding the molecular heterogeneity underlying tissue morphology. Trained on a pan-tissue dataset of 1.3 million paired H&amp;E-spatial patches across 25 organs and cancer types, H2O predicts spatial omics expression from histology with high concordance to sequenced measurements and consistently outperforms state-of-the-art models across three cancer benchmarks. Notably, H2O recovers the MIF-CD74/CD44 signaling axis directly from H&amp;E images, highlighting its capacity to infer biologically meaningful cell-cell communication without molecular profiling. [truncated]","doi":"10.64898/2026.04.21.717342","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: Methods > Datasets]\nHEST-1k. We first fine-tuned scGPT on a subset of the HEST-1k dataset to generate scGPT-FT. Using the same subset, we trained H2O to obtain its pretrained checkpoint. For benchmarking against other methods, we trained H2O on predefined HEST-1k subsets, yielding task-specific models H2O-ccRCC and H2O-PRAD. Model generalizability was evaluated using the HEST-1k-LymphNodeIDC cohort as an independent test set, comprising samples that were strictly withheld from both the scGPT fine-tuning and H2O training stages.\n\nThe HEST-1k dataset contains over 60,000 recognized gene symbols, with each sample capturing only a subset  of  these  genes  due  to  differences  in  sequencing  coverage,  gene  mapping  and  tissue  diversity.  To standardize feature space across samples, we identified the most variable genes within the dataset and selected the top 5,000. We then added genes sharing the same gene symbols with proteomic markers, resulting in a unified panel of 5,033 genes. This gene set was consistently used in both training and evaluation across all datasets.\n\nOpenST-Metastatic Lymph Node. This dataset contains 19 serial sections (S2-S38) from a single metastatic lymph node, each profiled by ST and matched H&amp;E images. We fine-tuned H2O-HEST-1k on section S2 and applied the model to predict expression across the remaining sections, enabling 3D construction of ST for the entire tissue.\n\nHuman Thymus Cell Atlas (HTSA). HTSA provides Visium-based ST together with H&amp;E images across fetal and early paediatric thymus samples spanning from 13 weeks post-fertilization to 2 years of age. At each time stage there are 2 or 4 samples except the 19-month-old stage and 19th week post-fertilization stage which each has only one sample. For both developmental stages, we used half of each time stage and the 19 th  week post-fertilization stage sample as training. The other half samples and the 19-month-old stage sample were used as test samples.\n\nHTAPP. The HTAPP dataset includes matched MERFISH transcriptomics, CODEX proteomics, and H&amp;E images from the same samples. This multi-omics resource was used to assess the generality of H2O across ST and SP. We applied Leave-One-Out-Validation (LOOV) on all eight multimodal H&amp;E-CODEX-MERFISH samples of the dataset.\n\n[input_representation: Methods > Model Training]\nWe first fine-tuned scGPT on a subset of HEST-1k to adapt its transcriptomics representations to ST context. Then, we trained a Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining (CLIP) -based foundation model on the HEST1k data pairs to align an in-house morphological FM with the fine-tuned scGPT (scGPT-FT), transforming the image FM into a bi-modal representation model that encodes both morphological and molecular information.\n\nFor each cell 𝑖 , we combine (1) an image embedding from a DINOv2-trained ViT, (2) a neighborhood context aggregated by a 1D convolution across 𝐾 adjacent patches, and (3) a conditioning signal from the spot  diameter  via  FiLM  to  generate  a  representation. The  resulting  representation  is  used  for  contrastive alignment with a text embedding ℎ 𝑖 and constructing the cell's gene-expression profiles of the cell.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000152","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Primary model paper proposing H2O for histopathology-to-spatial multi-omics inference. The evidence shows a text/LLM component and an explicit alignment bridge between histology and semantic molecular knowledge/text embeddings.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrates Vision Transformers (ViT) with Large Language Models (LLM) via contrastive learning\"; \"contrastive alignment with a text embedding h_i\"; \"bridges histopathology to spatial multi-omics\"; \"predict spatial omics expression from histology\"; \"constructing the cell's gene-expression profiles\"; \"candidate model\"? Actually evidence indicates a model, not a review or wrapper. However, the record is clearly a new foundation model framework named H2O. boundar_case clear_include, evidence_for_generative_model not_assessed, evidence_for_text_component \"LLM\" / \"text embedding h_i\", evidence_for_text_bio_bridge \"align histological morphology with semantic molecular knowledge\" and \"constructing the cell's gene-expression profiles\" , boundary_case \"clear_include\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000152","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record describes a generalist foundation model trained on 1.3M paired H&E-spatial patches across 25 organs and cancer types. The described task is direct inference/prediction of spatial omics from histology, with no explicit generative decoder or autoregressive generation mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"“H2O ... a generalist AI framework”; “Trained on a pan-tissue dataset of 1.3 million paired H&E-spatial patches across 25 organs and cancer types” / “predicts spatial omics expression from histology” / “contrastive alignment” / “constructing the cell's gene-expression profiles” / “transforms the image FM into a bi-modal representation model” / “evidence_for_text_component”: "},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"fallback_unresolved","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

